<div style="width: 100%; padding: 10px; background-color: #f0f8ff; border: 2px solid #1a5276; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Fun Outdoors!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Love to Run!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of simple sentences. Reading: Create a title or caption for a paragraph or image. Phonemic Awareness: Identify a spoken word's initial and final phoneme if these words have been previously encountered and are attached to a picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of words about outdoor activities in simple texts. (e.g., "She likes soccer."). Can create captions for pictures about outdoor games (e.g., "He runs fast."). Can recognize the initial and final sounds and letters of familiar spoken words by matching pictures to the correct sound and letter (e.g., identifying the initial /g/ sound and letter in "game" and the final /m/ sound when shown a picture of a game).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Let's Move!" TPR activity. The teacher says a phrase and students perform the action: "Run fast!" (students run in place quickly), "Jump high!" (students jump), "Swim slowly!" (students make swimming motions slowly), "Play soccer!" (students mimic kicking a ball). This reviews vocabulary and introduces verbs + adverbs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of previously learned outdoor activities (e.g., a child running, a child swimming, children playing a game). The teacher points and says, "I love to run fast!" while demonstrating the action, then points to a picture of swimming, "She swims slowly." The teacher emphasizes the /r/ sound in "run" and the /s/ sound in "swim" by exaggerating pronunciation. The teacher also shows a picture of a "game" and points to the letter 'G' and says "/g/, game".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions and gestures to check understanding.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(While demonstrating running fast) "Am I running fast or slow?" (Students point or say "Fast!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of swimming) "Is this 'run' or 'swim'?" (Students point or say "Swim!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple, illustrated narrative text titled "My Day at the Park." The text will consist of 3-4 simple sentences about children playing outdoors, incorporating target grammar (Subject + verb + adverb, Present Simple) and vocabulary (park, friend, run, fast, jump, high, play, soccer, ball, team, fun, like). Example text: "I love to play in the park. I play with my friend. We run fast and jump high! Sometimes, we play soccer with a big ball. I like my team! It's fun!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can understand very short, simple written sentences related to familiar topics, especially when accompanied by pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher reads the title and shows the pictures. "Is this story about playing inside or outside?" (Students point to 'outside' or say "Outside!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual Check):</b> The teacher reads a sentence from the text, then points to a picture. "The children play with a big ball. True or False?" (Students indicate thumbs up for true, thumbs down for false, referring to the illustration).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence-Picture Matching:</b> The teacher reads sentences from the text one by one. Students point to the corresponding picture in the story (e.g., "We run fast." - students point to the picture of children running).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of simple sentences (Subject + verb + adverb, Present simple). Vocabulary: nouns (park, friend, ball, team, game), verbs (run, jump, play, like, love), adjectives (big, fun), adverbs (fast, high). Phonemic awareness: identifying initial and final phonemes (/g/ in game, /m/ in swim, /r/ in run, /s/ in swim).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Read and Match:</b> Students receive a worksheet with three simple sentences from the "My Day at the Park" text on one side (e.g., "I love to play in the park.", "We run fast.", "They play soccer with a big ball.") and corresponding pictures on the other side. Students draw a line to match each sentence to its correct picture. The teacher circulates, offering help and monitoring.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion & Phonemes:</b> Students complete sentences with words from a small word bank (e.g., "We ___ fast." (run / swim / walk); "I like my ___!" (ball / team / friend)). Simultaneously, on the same worksheet, the teacher provides pictures of words previously encountered (e.g., a "game," "run," "swim"). Students identify and circle the initial letter (G for game, R for run) or the final letter (M for swim) corresponding to the sound they hear, reinforcing phonemic awareness.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs to compare their answers for the "Read and Match" and "Sentence Completion" activities. They take turns pointing to their chosen picture/word and reading the sentence aloud. The teacher encourages them to say, "I have (sentence/word). Do you have (sentence/word)?"</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The task connects directly to the Theme: "I Love to Run!" and Scenario: "Fun Outdoors!". Students will demonstrate reading comprehension by creating simple captions for new images of outdoor activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Caption This Picture!" Students are given a worksheet with three different pictures of children engaged in outdoor activities (e.g., a boy running very fast, a girl playing basketball, a group of friends jumping). Below each picture, there is space to write a caption or choose from a provided word bank/sentence bank.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the first picture (e.g., boy running fast) and models by reading two possible captions aloud: "He plays soccer." or "He runs fast!" The teacher guides students to choose the correct caption or helps them construct it orally.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then work individually or in pairs to create a simple caption (1-3 words or a simple sentence) for each picture, using previously learned vocabulary and grammar. (e.g., "He runs fast.", "She plays basketball.", "They jump high!", "It's fun!"). The teacher provides a word bank for support if needed (run, play, jump, fast, high, ball, friend, love, fun).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After writing, students take turns sharing their captions with a partner, reading them aloud. The teacher walks around, listens, and offers corrections and encouragement, focusing on reading comprehension and accurate word use.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading the captions, collaborative interaction (if done in pairs), and communicative task completion (successful captioning). This activity reinforces the ability to relate simple sentences to images.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and a short individual task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a worksheet with two parts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1 (Reading Comprehension):</b> A picture depicting an outdoor activity (e.g., children playing baseball). Below the picture, there is a very short paragraph (1-2 sentences) about the activity (e.g., "We play baseball. It is a fun sport."). Students must read the paragraph and choose the best title for it from two options (e.g., a) "My Ball", b) "Playing Baseball").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2 (Phonemic Awareness):</b> Three small pictures (e.g., 'game', 'run', 'swim'). Students are asked to draw a circle around the letter that starts the word "game" (G) and draw a square around the letter that ends the word "swim" (M).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly chooses the title or caption for the paragraph/image (showing comprehension of simple sentences).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies the initial and final phonemes with their letter correspondence in familiar words.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary appropriately in captions (if writing).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read words about outdoor activities." (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like to read about playing outside." (Draw: Happy face / Neutral face / Sad face)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new thing you could read today? (e.g., "run fast," "play soccer").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did you like most about reading the stories about playing outside?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading comprehension lesson? (e.g., students were engaged with the illustrated text, matching activities were effective).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially for students who struggled with simple sentence comprehension or phonemic awareness? (e.g., provide more guided practice with sentence structure, use more explicit phonics instruction).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the explicit focus on reading skills from today be effectively connected to the speaking and interaction focus of Lesson 3? (e.g., encourage students to orally describe the pictures they read about today).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The reading comprehension of simple sentences and vocabulary established in this lesson will directly support students' ability to speak and interact about outdoor activities in Lesson 3, where they will use these words and structures to express preferences and ask questions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>